      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im working on a schema and its currently in 2nf but its getting kinda messy with redundant data. can u walk me through how to get it to 3nf? specifically what im looking for is how to handle the transitive dependencies so i dont have to update stuff in multiple places",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! In order to convert your schema from 2NF to 3NF, you need to eliminate transitive dependencies where non-key attributes depend on other non-key attributes. Here's how you can do it:\n\n1. Identify functional dependencies: Go through your schema and understand which attributes are functionally dependent on each other.\n\n2. Detect transitive dependencies: Transitive dependencies occur when a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute, not on the primary key. For example, if A -> B and B -> C, then C is transitively dependent on A.\n\n3. Decompose your tables: Once you have identified the transitive dependencies, you can start splitting your tables into smaller ones. The goal is to create child relationships where non-key attributes are only dependent on the primary key, not on any other non-key attributes.\n\n4. Reassign attributes carefully: As you create child tables, you must carefully examine all the attributes and reassign them to the respective tables. Sometimes, you may even need to break down a table into multiple smaller tables.\n\n5. Check for redundancies: After decomposing your tables, ensure that there are no redundant data or paths to update data in multiple places. Re-examine your schema to ensure that",
